UX-EP-001 — BTA User Experience Standards

Document ID: UX-EP-001
Title: Bible Therapy Assistant™ User Experience Standards
Status: Draft – Phase 2A
Owner: BTA Platform Architecture
Applies To: All BTA user interaction workflows

1. Purpose

This document establishes the User Experience (UX) standards governing how readers interact with Bible Therapy Assistant™ (BTA).

Its purpose is to ensure every interaction is intuitive, consistent, accessible, and supportive of Scripture-centered exploration while preserving the constitutional principles governing the platform.

This document governs interaction workflows.

2. Scope

UX standards include, but are not limited to:

Reader journeys
Navigation flow
Scripture search workflow
Progressive Disclosure
Contact workflow
Connect workflow
Donation workflow
Error recovery
Success confirmation
Expand/collapse behavior
Modal and workspace interactions
Mobile interaction patterns
Accessibility interactions
3. Relationship Between UX and UI

Although User Experience (UX) and User Interface (UI) are complementary disciplines, they govern different aspects of the BTA platform.

User Experience (UX)

User Experience governs how readers accomplish objectives within BTA.

Examples include:

Performing a Scripture search
Connecting with BTA
Contacting the ministry
Making a donation
Navigating Progressive Disclosure
Receiving confirmations
Recovering from errors

UX answers the question:

"How does the reader accomplish their objective?"

User Interface (UI)

User Interface governs how those workflows are visually presented.

Examples include:

Navigation layout
Typography
Colors
Buttons and links
Responsive presentation
Component styling

UI answers the question:

"How is the workflow presented?"

4. Governance Principle

UX standards govern interaction.

UI standards govern presentation.

Neither document shall redefine the responsibilities of the other.

Both work together to produce a consistent, accessible reader experience.

5. Reader Experience Objectives

Every workflow shall strive to achieve the following objectives:

Minimize unnecessary steps.
Maintain a consistent interaction model.
Provide clear feedback after every significant action.
Preserve reader orientation throughout navigation.
Support both desktop and mobile interaction naturally.
Reduce cognitive load.
Encourage continued engagement with Scripture.
Avoid unnecessary interruptions during reflection.
6. Interaction Standards

All user interactions should provide clear and predictable behavior.

Desktop
Hover provides visual confirmation.
Click executes the selected action.
Mobile
Tap executes the selected action.
Workflows should require no hover-dependent behavior.
Keyboard Accessibility
Navigation shall support keyboard traversal.
Focus indicators shall clearly identify the active element.
Enter or Space shall execute the selected action where appropriate.
7. Progressive Disclosure

BTA shall present information incrementally, allowing readers to choose when additional detail is desired.

Progressive Disclosure shall:

Reduce information overload.
Preserve readability.
Encourage exploration.
Support varying levels of reader interest.
Maintain continuity throughout the reader journey.
8. Reader Journey Principles

Every workflow should reinforce why the reader came to BTA.

Interactions should promote:

Trust
Calmness
Clarity
Reflection
Confidence
Accessibility

Interface behavior should never compete with Scripture for the reader's attention.

9. Future Workflow Governance

Future workflow standards may include:

About BTA
Contact Workspace
Connect Workspace
Donation Workspace
Reader Account
Saved Studies
Search History
Notifications
Audience Engagement
Subscription Management

Each workflow shall comply with the principles established in this document.

